From b33e382a92152026b1de58df2c28f282a48f8a9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 07:00:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Seeing further: the new look's view reaches 1200 yd by
 default and up to 2000
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

1.12 stops the detailed world at `farclip` (177 to 777 yd, 350 by default), and Elwynn's own fog
ends near 500 yd by day, so raising one without the other shows nothing. Under the new look the
world's reach is mapped from the same `farclip`: 350 (the default) reaches 1200 yd, the slider's
top (777) 2000, its bottom (177) 600, linear between. The 1.12 Video Options slider keeps working
over its whole travel under either look, and the `farclip` CVar keeps the reference's range and
refusal, so the classic look is untouched. Elwynn's grade (0051) scales its fog end out by time of
day (×2.5 at noon) to meet the longer view, so the far hills come out of the haze.

Everything the far wall bounds follows the reach: the per-pixel wall and the fog's cap, the CPU
cull of doodads and WMOs, the WMO interior fog's clamp, the particle draw set and the terrain
residency window, which widens by the reference's own formula (±39 chunks, at most 6×6 tiles, at
1200 yd; ±63 chunks, at most 9×9, at 2000; 5×5 at the classic 777). The art caches' sweep never
undercuts the wider block. The camera's far plane (3000) and the WDL horizon ring (5 tiles) already
cover 2000 yd. Small props already fade out by size well before the wall (the reference's
size-bucketed fade), so nothing new pops in the distance.
